@vsm 1.0
// docs/incidents/2026-08-02_recovery_plan.vsm
// RECOVERY PLAN — companion to
// docs/incidents/2026-08-02_clima_nats_observability_incident.vsm.
// Built live with rmw3 2026-08-03, after the incident's identification/
// containment/eradication phases closed. This is the phase_recovery gate
// content that incident report deferred.
// Status: PLAN, not yet executed beyond what's marked done below.

plan(POST-INCIDENT-RECOVERY-2026-08-03) {

  rmw3_framing: "no basta con observar la brecha. necesitamos
      retroalimentar el modelo para que complete su función cibernética.
      esto es asignar las correspondientes señales telemetricas al
      nucleo. probablemente haya una nueva fase de calibración.
      [...] cablear todas las variables a un único archivo de
      configuración [...] antes de abrir un s1 nuevamente vamos a probar
      y exigir al computador, con pruebas de límite [...] una vez
      pasemos un umbral de certificación, abrimos los s1.",

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // PHASE B — CLOSE THE OBSERVABILITY GAP (sense AND act, not just alert)
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  phase_B_cybernetic_loop_closure: {
    reframe: "The original STAGE2B/2C/2D scope (docs/spec/sensor_omega_
        integration/spec_2026-08-02.vsm section 3) was framed as
        'classify each disconnected signal into a-e buckets, mostly
        landing in (c) single-loop-algedonic or (e) passive-audit'. rmw3
        sharpens this: passive classification into an alert bucket is
        still just OBSERVING the gap. Completing the cybernetic function
        means signals that belong in classification (a) [Omega dimension]
        or (b) [S3-bargain input] must actually be WIRED INTO the
        nucleus's real decision loop -- not just logged or algedonic'd,
        but consumed by S3's bargain function / the state vector itself,
        so the system's own behavior changes in response, closing
        sense->decide->act->sense.",

    B1_stage2c_generic_liveness_scanner: "Unchanged from the original
        spec -- NRestarts/ActiveState scanner, classification (c),
        single-loop-authorized per A5/D4. Still the right first build,
        cheapest/highest-coverage. Status: queued.",

    B2_stage2d_reclassification_PLUS_wiring: "Extends the original
        STAGE2D (hardware inventory reclassification against the a-e
        taxonomy) with an explicit SECOND step for anything that lands in
        (a)/(b): don't stop at 'this belongs in the S3 bargain' -- actually
        implement the wiring (new state-vector dimension, or a new input
        to vsf-s3-alloc's bargain function) so the nucleus's real
        allocation/governance behavior changes. Status: queued, now with
        broader scope than originally written.",

    B3_new_calibration_phase: "rmw3's addition, not in the original spec.
        Once new signals are wired into Omega/the state vector/S3's
        bargain, the system's calibration (thresholds, r_core, Fierro
        Q1-Q3 validity -- Omega convex / L<1 / sigma_max, per
        [[project_fierro_protocol]] / calibration.rs) needs to be
        RE-RUN, not assumed to still hold. Adding a dimension or a
        bargain input changes the geometry Fierro validates. This should
        follow the SAME protocol already used for onboarding any new VSF
        domain (Q1/Q2/Q3 in calibration.rs) -- not a new, separate
        calibration methodology, an APPLICATION of the existing one to
        the newly-wired signals. Status: not yet scoped in detail --
        depends on which signals B2 actually wires in.",
  },

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // PHASE C-EXTENDED — CODE CLEANUP + CONFIG CENTRALIZATION
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  phase_C_config_centralization: {
    rmw3_ask: "limpieza de todo el codigo que no es util + optimizar
        codigo ambiguo. cablear TODAS las variables a un unico archivo
        de configuracion -- igual que con el token NATS, no recablear
        todo a mano, cambiar un parametro en un lugar central.",
    generalizes_the_nats_token_lesson: "2026-08-03's rotation (see
        docs/spec/nats_token_registry.vsm) found the SAME token scattered
        across 19+ locations in 6 different file-format conventions
        (systemd Environment=, EnvironmentFiles=, cron.d, hardcoded Python
        fallbacks, a bare .token file, nats-server.conf) -- and TWO of
        those locations were missed on the first sweep, nearly leaving a
        component silently broken. rmw3's ask is the general fix: this
        shouldn't be specific to the NATS token, it should be true of
        every fleet-wide config value.",
    connects_to_existing_work: "This is NOT a brand-new idea --
        [[project_s1_isolation_relay_arch]] already scoped 'config-canon
        consolidation: state.db becomes the single GENERATIVE source for
        IP/VLAN/firewall/coupling config, derived artifacts (.fw,
        systemd-embedded iptables, interfaces, CLAUDE.md table) get
        generated not hand-maintained' -- for network config specifically.
        rmw3 is generalizing that same principle to secrets/tokens/env
        vars broadly. These should probably be ONE consolidation effort,
        not two parallel ones -- state.db (or a new dedicated secrets
        table alongside it) as the single generative source, with a
        deploy step that PUSHES the current value out to every registered
        location (matching scripts/state/rotate_nats_token.sh's shape,
        generalized beyond just the NATS token).",
    dead_code_cleanup: "Separate but bundled per rmw3's ask: sweep for
        genuinely unused code (the bbs.rs removal earlier tonight is the
        template) and ambiguous/duplicated logic. Candidate targets
        already known from tonight: the .claude/worktrees/fix-a0-
        classifier-chain worktree (stale branch, needs a decision --
        resync or remove), any other dead modules similar to bbs.rs found
        along the way.",
    status: "Not scoped into concrete tasks yet -- this plan entry is the
        registration, not an implementation spec. Needs its own design
        pass (what's the config schema, where does it live, what's the
        push mechanism) before dispatching to DS.",
  },

  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  // PHASE D — CERTIFICATION GATE (stress-test BEFORE reopening any S1)
  // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

  phase_D_certification_before_s1_reintegration: {
    rmw3_ask: "antes de abrir un s1 nuevamente, probar y exigir al
        computador con pruebas de limite: mecanismo de asignacion de
        recursos, senales de alerta, politicas, etc. Un umbral de
        certificacion, y recien ahi abrimos los s1.",
    codified_as: "RULES.md R14 -- 'Declared behavior is not demonstrated
        behavior.' This phase is R14's concrete enforcement point, not a
        standalone idea -- read R14 first for the full rationale (3
        independently-confirmed same-session instances of claimed-vs-real
        divergence: service_watchdog's silent publish failure,
        vsf-s3-star's fake 'connected' log, systemd's Restart=always
        masking a 22h crash-loop).",
    reframe: "Recovery isn't 'wait until things look stable and then
        reopen' -- it's an active, adversarial certification step. Don't
        passively observe; deliberately stress the system's resilience
        mechanisms and require them to demonstrably work before trusting
        them with real S1 load again.",
    connects_to_existing_work: "vsf-auditd / recovery_exercise.py
        already implement exactly this pattern at a small scale (gates
        g2-g5, e.g. g3: kill NATS on 106, verify subscriber PIDs survive
        AND the chain head keeps advancing). This phase generalizes that
        into a deliberate, comprehensive PRE-RECOVERY battery rather than
        the existing Poisson-thinned background sampling -- run all
        relevant gates on demand, add new ones covering what this
        incident specifically exposed (NATS auth failure recovery, S3
        resource-bargain response under simulated pressure, algedonic
        alert delivery end-to-end under a real induced fault, not just a
        clean connectivity check).",
    candidate_test_battery: [
      "Kill NATS auth (wrong token) on a client -- verify it reconnects
       cleanly once corrected, verify an algedonic alert actually fires
       AND is delivered (not just logged) within a bounded time.",
      "Saturate LXC 106's connection/fd budget synthetically -- verify
       the generic liveness scanner (B1) catches it and the system
       responds per its classified action (single-loop restart, or
       escalation) rather than silently degrading like tonight.",
      "Kill a service under S3's resource bargain (vsf-s3-alloc) --
       verify grants reallocate sanely, no crash-loop, no silent
       resource-starvation of a sibling.",
      "Full recovery_exercise.py g2-g5 battery run on-demand (not waiting
       for the Poisson timer) as a final pre-certification pass.",
    ],
    certification_threshold: "NOT YET DEFINED -- needs an explicit,
        written pass/fail criterion (e.g. 'all gates PASS, zero
        unexplained algedonic silence, generic liveness scanner correctly
        flags 100% of injected faults') before this phase can actually
        gate anything. Registering the NEED for a threshold, not
        proposing one yet -- that's a design decision for when phases B/C
        are further along and we know what's actually being certified.",
    status: "Not started. Explicitly the LAST gate before S1
        reintegration -- sequenced after B and C are reasonably complete,
        not run against today's still-in-flux state.",
  },

  sequencing: "B1 (generic liveness scanner) is buildable now, independent
      of everything else. B2/B3 (wiring + calibration) and C (config
      centralization) are bigger design efforts needing their own
      dedicated passes, not something to start at the tail end of an
      already very long session. D (certification) is explicitly LAST,
      gates S1 reintegration, and can't be meaningfully run until B/C
      exist to be certified. rmw3 to decide pacing across future
      sessions -- this plan is the registration of scope, not a
      commitment to do it all now.",
}
